package example_charts;

import common.ExampleNotes;

/** Demonstrator notes of GemBarChartExample (see common.ExampleNotes). */
component GemBarChartNotes() {
  @ExampleNotes(
    title = "Bar Chart",
    docComponents = ["GemBarChart"],
    about = [
      "What you see.|The persons of the demo database counted in three age groups (born before 1993, between 1993 and 1998, after 1998), each group split into people with at most two friends and people with more. stacked = false puts the two bars of a group side by side; the configuration panel of the chart lets you switch.",
      "Where the data comes from.|In the model the chart's data is null. The chart instance is named (barchart@GemBarChart), and MontiGem generates a getter for every parameter of a named instance - getBarchartData() - which the Java class of the page overrides to build a GemBarChartData from the persons. This is the usual division of labour: the model declares what is shown, the Java class computes it.",
      "Backend variant.|The pages GemBarChartFromCommand (not in the navigation, route /example_charts/GemBarChartFromCommand) get the same chart data from a backend command instead: the service method App.getBarChart declared in the class diagram."
    ],
    tryIt = [
      "Open \"Show Java\": getBarchartData() walks the persons and increments the counters of the right group.",
      "Change a birthday in the Table example (category \"Arrange\") and come back: the bars move.",
      "Use the chart's own configuration panel to stack the bars or change the value axis."
    ]
  );
}
